WEBVTT

00:00:00.267 --> 00:00:04.533
The first week's scoreboard asked
for a real data source on day one.

00:00:04.533 --> 00:00:09.700
If your book lives in a spreadsheet,
this is how it gets in: companies,

00:00:09.700 --> 00:00:13.033
contacts and deals, linked to
each other, in one afternoon.

00:00:13.100 --> 00:00:15.767
You'll import a CSV with a company column

00:00:15.767 --> 00:00:19.233
and read the match report
before anything is written.

00:00:19.233 --> 00:00:22.767
Then you'll shape the
organization with custom objects,

00:00:22.767 --> 00:00:24.700
fields and pipelines.

00:00:24.733 --> 00:00:27.867
Leo imports a contact file the usual way.

00:00:27.867 --> 00:00:32.767
A typo in one column creates a
duplicate account, then another,

00:00:32.767 --> 00:00:36.567
and by month end the
duplicates have no author.

00:00:36.633 --> 00:00:40.167
Three rules prevent that.
Preview writes nothing.

00:00:40.167 --> 00:00:42.933
Matching never creates a company.

00:00:42.933 --> 00:00:47.600
And importing again updates what
exists, instead of duplicating it.

00:00:47.633 --> 00:00:51.833
Step one, prepare the file. One
header row, one record per line.

00:00:51.833 --> 00:00:54.400
If you have the company
website, include it,

00:00:54.400 --> 00:00:56.467
because websites are matched first.

00:00:56.467 --> 00:01:00.067
Keep a copy of the original,
so you can always start again.

00:01:00.133 --> 00:01:03.833
Step two, choose and map. In
the console: CRM, then Import.

00:01:03.833 --> 00:01:07.167
There are four steps: choose,
map, preview and commit.

00:01:07.167 --> 00:01:10.567
Choose what you're importing,
companies, contacts or deals,

00:01:10.567 --> 00:01:14.900
then add the file. Committing is
for owners and admins by default.

00:01:14.900 --> 00:01:17.967
An owner can let members
commit in the CRM settings.

00:01:18.033 --> 00:01:19.067
Map your columns.

00:01:19.067 --> 00:01:23.200
Each column goes to a field, to a
custom field you made, or is left out.

00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:26.200
For contacts and deals,
map one column to Company.

00:01:26.200 --> 00:01:29.533
GTM OS links each row to an
existing company, by website first,

00:01:29.533 --> 00:01:32.400
then by exact name. It never
creates a company for you,

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:35.700
so a typo can't spawn a duplicate account.

00:01:35.733 --> 00:01:40.600
A yes or no column accepts yes or
no, true or false, or one or zero.

00:01:40.600 --> 00:01:42.800
A number column takes plain numbers.

00:01:42.800 --> 00:01:46.467
Rows are matched on a key, such
as the domain for companies.

00:01:46.467 --> 00:01:50.200
So importing the file
again updates what exists.

00:01:50.267 --> 00:01:53.100
Step three, preview, where
nothing is written yet.

00:01:53.100 --> 00:01:56.567
Every row is classified as create,
update, duplicate or warning.

00:01:56.567 --> 00:01:59.600
The company card shows three
counts: rows that matched,

00:01:59.600 --> 00:02:03.667
rows that were ambiguous because
more than one company fits,

00:02:03.667 --> 00:02:05.367
and rows that found no company at all.

00:02:05.433 --> 00:02:08.500
The problem rows are listed
with their file line,

00:02:08.500 --> 00:02:11.067
and those rows import
without a company link.

00:02:11.067 --> 00:02:13.367
Long lists are shortened,
but the counts are exact.

00:02:13.367 --> 00:02:17.733
Fix the file and preview again. Operator
tip: preview a ten row slice first.

00:02:17.733 --> 00:02:20.633
It costs nothing, because
preview writes nothing.

00:02:20.667 --> 00:02:23.200
Step four, commit. Rows go in batches.

00:02:23.200 --> 00:02:26.133
A file with a company column
goes in batches of two hundred,

00:02:26.133 --> 00:02:29.100
and the page shows which batch it's on.

00:02:29.100 --> 00:02:31.967
A large file takes a few
minutes, so keep the page open.

00:02:31.967 --> 00:02:35.533
If one batch fails, the batches
already written stay written,

00:02:35.533 --> 00:02:38.300
and the result shows the
real outcome for each row.

00:02:38.333 --> 00:02:42.333
The result also counts how many
company links were made, already there,

00:02:42.333 --> 00:02:44.533
or failed. And an import starts nothing.

00:02:44.533 --> 00:02:48.200
Event plays and agent triggers
skip bulk writes by default,

00:02:48.200 --> 00:02:51.500
so a big file won't fire a
first touch for every row.

00:02:51.567 --> 00:02:56.700
An import writes only to your own
organization and sends nothing outside it.

00:02:56.700 --> 00:02:58.433
If something lands wrong,

00:02:58.433 --> 00:03:02.067
archive the record from its
page and restore it later.

00:03:02.067 --> 00:03:04.733
Purging for good needs an approval.

00:03:04.767 --> 00:03:06.967
Or ask your agent: "Import contacts.

00:03:06.967 --> 00:03:10.600
csv into Contacts, link each contact
to its company, preview first,

00:03:10.600 --> 00:03:13.500
and show me which
companies did not match".

00:03:13.500 --> 00:03:16.233
It previews, shows the match counts,

00:03:16.233 --> 00:03:18.067
and lists the companies that didn't match.

00:03:18.067 --> 00:03:21.333
You decide what to fix, and
it commits when you say so.

00:03:21.367 --> 00:03:25.867
Step five, shape it to your business.
In the console: CRM, then Model.

00:03:25.867 --> 00:03:29.267
Define a custom object, for example
a Partner, with its fields: text,

00:03:29.267 --> 00:03:32.733
a number, a list of allowed values,
or a reference to another object.

00:03:32.733 --> 00:03:36.867
Validate it, then publish. Publishing
is proposed as a ChangeSet.

00:03:36.867 --> 00:03:38.967
Another owner or admin approves it,

00:03:38.967 --> 00:03:41.367
so a change to your model is never silent.

00:03:41.433 --> 00:03:44.833
For extra fields on companies,
contacts and deals: Settings,

00:03:44.833 --> 00:03:46.333
then Custom fields.

00:03:46.333 --> 00:03:49.933
Pick the object, name the field,
choose a type, and set its options:

00:03:49.933 --> 00:03:53.300
required, show on record,
allow the agent to fill.

00:03:53.300 --> 00:03:57.267
Fields can be archived and restored,
so a wrong turn is reversible.

00:03:57.333 --> 00:04:00.300
One honest note. Show in tables
and Allow filtering are saved,

00:04:00.300 --> 00:04:04.133
but the Companies and Contacts
lists don't use them yet.

00:04:04.133 --> 00:04:06.200
Those columns and filters
arrive in a later release.

00:04:06.200 --> 00:04:10.067
Today the field already works in
records list filters and for your agent.

00:04:10.067 --> 00:04:13.067
You can ask for every company
where it equals a value.

00:04:13.133 --> 00:04:16.533
Step six, pipelines. In the
console: Settings, then Pipelines.

00:04:16.533 --> 00:04:19.567
Create a deal pipeline
and name its stages.

00:04:19.567 --> 00:04:21.967
Give each stage a category,
open, won or lost,

00:04:21.967 --> 00:04:24.800
and a default win
probability. Drag to reorder.

00:04:24.800 --> 00:04:27.367
The page refuses what would strand deals,

00:04:27.367 --> 00:04:30.267
such as archiving a stage
that still holds open deals.

00:04:30.267 --> 00:04:33.600
Archived stages and
pipelines can be restored.

00:04:33.633 --> 00:04:35.167
Everything here has an agent path.

00:04:35.167 --> 00:04:39.500
Ask your agent: "Add a Partner object
with the fields tier and region".

00:04:39.500 --> 00:04:42.600
It proposes the change and
returns an approval number,

00:04:42.600 --> 00:04:45.267
and you or a teammate
approve it in Approvals.

00:04:45.267 --> 00:04:49.367
Then ask: "Create a deal pipeline called
Partnerships with the stages Intro,

00:04:49.367 --> 00:04:51.433
Active and Signed". It creates them.

00:04:51.500 --> 00:04:55.233
The worry: one bad file could
wreck my CRM. It can't do much.

00:04:55.233 --> 00:04:59.033
Preview writes nothing, and
matching never creates a company.

00:04:59.033 --> 00:05:01.400
A failed batch doesn't undo the rest,

00:05:01.400 --> 00:05:06.067
and anything that lands wrong is archived
and restored from its record page.

00:05:06.100 --> 00:05:11.500
Run it back: Prepare, map, preview,
commit. Then model your business.

00:05:11.500 --> 00:05:15.167
Preview writes nothing, matching
never creates a company,

00:05:15.167 --> 00:05:18.167
and re-importing never duplicates.

00:05:18.200 --> 00:05:24.233
Your records are in, linked to their
companies and shaped to your business.

00:05:24.233 --> 00:05:30.067
Next: your CRM, kept in sync, so the
tool you already use stays the record.
